# The four secrets, for local development. Copy to `.dev.vars` (gitignored) and fill in.
# On the deployed Worker the same four go in one at a time with `wrangler secret put <NAME>`.
#
# Not here: the race, the block and the goal — those are not secret and live in `.env`
# (see `.env.example`), and the Strava *client id*, which is public and sits in
# `wrangler.jsonc`. Full walkthrough: docs/setup.md.

# The one-time bootstrap secret, and nothing else. It no longer signs anyone in: it
# authorises the single `POST /api/bootstrap` that gives the owner account its first email
# and password, after which it is spent. Everyone else arrives on an invitation.
APP_PASSWORD=""

# From your own Strava application at https://www.strava.com/settings/api → Client Secret.
# Every deployment uses its own application; the client id in wrangler.jsonc is the
# author's and is bound to the author's callback domain. See docs/setup.md §b.
STRAVA_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# Any random string you invent. Strava echoes it back once, when you create the webhook
# subscription, and the callback compares it in constant time before accepting one.
# `openssl rand -hex 16` is as good as anything.
STRAVA_WEBHOOK_VERIFY=""

# AES-GCM key for the Strava refresh tokens at rest, so a database dump is not a stolen
# Strava account — one per connected athlete, all under this key. Must decode to exactly
# 32 bytes: `openssl rand -base64 32`. Treat it as permanent: regenerate it on a live
# deployment and every stored token stops decrypting, so every athlete has to reconnect.
TOKEN_ENC_KEY=""

# Signs the session cookies. Not a password anyone types, and never `APP_PASSWORD`: a
# value a person types must not double as the key every device's cookie hangs from.
# `openssl rand -base64 32`. Rotating it signs every athlete out of every device.
SESSION_SECRET=""
